//
// Conduit (--use-conduit --conduit-fuse-spatial --conduit-fuse-channels-flag)
// end-to-end run for the COMPOSITION of `--conduit-fuse-spatial` followed by
// `--conduit-fuse-channels`.  Sibling-pair to the single-pass smokes:
//   test/npu-xrt/fuse_operators_basic_npu/conduit.lit
//   test/npu-xrt/fuse_channels_npu/conduit.lit
// And companion to:
//   test/npu-xrt/fuse_channels_after_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit  (composition
//                                              #1: core-bodies + channels)
//
// Why a separate composition fixture: fuse_operators_basic_npu hand-authors
// the two-device single-output-per-core shape that fuse-spatial needs as
// INPUT (one producer-side conduit per core post-merge → no fuse-channels
// work).  fuse_channels_npu hand-authors the single-device
// two-producer-side-conduits-on-one-tile shape that fuse-channels needs as
// INPUT.  Neither answers the production-pipeline question: does
// fuse-spatial's OUTPUT contain a topology where fuse-channels can find
// eligible groups?  This fixture extends the basic-spatial source IR with a
// SECOND producer-side output on the consumer core (`@ext_out_aux`) so
// post-spatial there are TWO producer-side conduits co-located on the
// merged consumer tile in disjoint windows — exactly the eligibility
// surface fuse-channels groups.  Companion to
// `fuse_channels_after_core_bodies_npu` (whose topology degraded to
// fuse-channels no-op on the cloned core-bodies source IR — see that
// fixture's lit:60-69 cross-reference).
//
// Source IR (see aie.mlir header for full context): TWO `aie.device` blocks
// (devAdd + devMul), both targeting `tile(0,2)` pre-fuse, intermediate
// channel pair (`@inter_add` on devAdd; `@consume_add` on devMul) tagged
// with matching `fusion_group="addmul_spatial_chan"`.  devMul's core has
// TWO sequential sections inside its outer `scf.for`:
//
//   Section A: acquire @consume_add (Consume), acquire @ext_out_mul
//              (Produce), inline `mul %v, 2.0`, release both.
//   Section B: acquire @ext_out_aux (Produce), inline write
//              `arith.constant 7.0 : bf16` to all 64 elements, release.
//
// `--conduit-fuse-spatial` injects `--conduit-fuse-operators` per
// `aiecc.cpp:332` AND triggers `aie-combine-device same-tile=false` (the
// default routing without `--conduit-fuse-core-bodies-flag`), collapsing
// the two devices into ONE merged device with devMul's tiles offset to
// column 1 — merged producer core sits at tile(0,2), merged consumer core
// at tile(1,2).  The intermediate conduit pair is replaced by a single
// `@fused_intermediate_0` cross-column on-chip channel.
//
// `--conduit-fuse-channels` then runs on the merged-device IR.  Producer-
// side conduits on the merged consumer core tile(1,2): `@ext_out_mul` and
// `@ext_out_aux`, sequential in the same outer-`scf.for` body, with
// disjoint windows (Section A's `@ext_out_mul` Produce-release strictly
// precedes Section B's `@ext_out_aux` Produce-acquire).  Per
// `ConduitFuseChannels.cpp:303-399` (device-walk, tile-grouped
// producer-side conduits, per-block greedy interval coloring), the pass
// stamps `dma_channel_group="groupN"` on both — fuse-channels finds
// eligible work post-spatial.  Producer core on tile(0,2) has only one
// producer-side conduit (`@fused_intermediate_0`) — single-member group is
// a no-op on that tile.  Two-column smoke uses `npu2_2col`.
//
// OBSERVED OUTCOME (2026-05-01, recorded post aie-opt validation):
// fuse-channels successfully groups @ext_out_mul + @ext_out_aux on the
// merged consumer tile post-spatial.  This fixture's HW value is therefore
// the STRONGER "fuse-channels-with-work-post-merge" signal — directly
// complementing `fuse_channels_after_core_bodies_npu`'s observed weaker
// (no-op) signal on the cloned core-bodies source IR.
//
// Why both `--conduit-fuse-spatial` AND `--conduit-fuse-channels-flag` (no
// `--conduit-fuse-core-bodies-flag`): `--conduit-fuse-spatial` already
// routes through `--aie-combine-device same-tile=false` (the default
// routing without the core-bodies flag), which is what collapses the two
// pre-fuse devices and offsets devMul to column 1.  The core-bodies flag
// is intentionally OFF — its inclusion would route through
// `--aie-combine-device same-tile=true` (per `aiecc.cpp:1509-1511`),
// collapsing both cores onto the same tile, which is a DIFFERENT
// composition tested by Task #47's full hybrid fixture.
//
// Reference compute: `out_mul[j] = (in_a[j] + 1.0) * 2.0`,
// `in_a[j] = (j % 16)`, `out_aux[j] = 7.0` (constant, all elements) —
// bf16-exact (max value 32 < 256, 7.0 < 256).  NUM_INVOCATIONS=4,
// IO_LEN=64.
//
// PASS expectation: every dispatch (4 invocations) completes; output bytes
// for both outputs match reference computed by test.cpp, and (separately,
// by construction) the out_mul bytes match the basic-spatial sibling on
// the same input.  Failure modes that single-pass smokes cannot catch:
//   * Compile-time crash inside fuse-channels triggered by fuse-spatial's
//     output IR shape (the question this fixture answers).
//   * fuse-channels mis-grouping the surviving producer-side conduits on
//     the merged consumer tile — silent wrong-bytes output where
//     out_mul-destined bytes land in bo_aux (or vice versa) via a swapped
//     DMA channel.
//   * Multi-section re-acquire stability of grouped channels reached via
//     spatial fusion (vs. hand-authored single-device eligibility tested
//     by fuse_channels_npu).
//
// Sibling references:
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_operators_basic_npu/conduit.lit` — single-pass
//     fuse-spatial 1:1 with single output per core.  Same Add+Mul reference
//     compute.
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_channels_npu/conduit.lit` — single-pass
//     fuse-channels with hand-authored single-device single-core
//     eligibility.  Same Add+Mul reference compute.
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_channels_after_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit` —
//     adjacent composition (core-bodies + channels) on same-tile=true
//     routing, observed weaker no-op outcome.  This fixture is the
//     stronger-signal companion on same-tile=false routing.
//   * `test/Dialect/Conduit/path_c_async_fuse_corebody_blocks_at_wait_all.mlir`
//     — XFAIL pin for the related MED bug (fusion-then-Pass-C IR with
//     `wait_all` consumers).  This fixture deliberately avoids `wait_all`
//     to skirt that XFAIL'd path.
//
// No stateful companion: the source IR uses Conduit-only cross-device
// fusion semantics (`fusion_group` annotation + `--conduit-fuse-spatial` +
// `--conduit-fuse-channels` `dma_channel_group` annotation) that have no
// stateful objectfifo equivalent.  Reference is computed in test.cpp.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai, peano
//
// Kernel compute is inlined bf16 add + multiply + constant store.  Chess
// (xchesscc) cannot select `fp_to_bf16` for this inline core (`fatal error:
// error in backend: Cannot select: i32 = fp_to_bf16`); Peano can.  Use
// `--no-xchesscc --no-xbridge` to route the kernel compile through Peano
// (sibling pattern: `fuse_operators_basic_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `fuse_channels_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `fuse_channels_after_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `fuse_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `fuse_operators_convergent_npu/conduit.lit`, `loadpdi/run.lit`,
// `vec_vec_add_objfifo_init/aie2.py`).
//
// RUN: cp %S/aie.mlir aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% sed 's/NPUDEVICE/npu2_2col/g' -i aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% %python aiecc.py --no-aiesim --no-xchesscc --no-xbridge --aie-generate-xclbin --aie-generate-npu-insts --no-compile-host --use-conduit --conduit-fuse-spatial --conduit-fuse-channels-flag --xclbin-name=aie.xclbin --npu-insts-name=insts.bin aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% clang %S/test.cpp -o test.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags -lrt -lstdc++ %test_utils_flags
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% ./test.exe -x aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i insts.bin
